   "text": {
    "L1": "A conductor lets electric current pass easily. An insulator does not let current pass, or hardly at all.",
    "L2": "A simple tester uses a cell, a bulb, and two wires with a gap.",
    "L3": "Touch the free wire ends to a material. If the bulb glows, the material completes the circuit and is a conductor.",
    "L4": "A steel hairpin makes the bulb glow, but a plastic button does not.",
    "L5": "Copper, aluminium, and iron are good conductors. Graphite also conducts, even though it is a non-metal.",
    "L6": "Plastic, rubber, wood, glass, paper, and dry cloth are insulators.",
    "L7": "Copper or aluminium inside a wire carries current. Plastic outside insulates it and helps keep us safe.",
    "L8": "Tap water may not light a torch bulb, but it conducts weakly. Wet hands and mains electricity are dangerous, so never use appliances with wet hands."
   },
